-- title:
--   Points of the Taylor–Wiles Hecke ring: eigenform or Eisenstein
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation ring (a domain, adically complete for its maximal ideal, with finite residue field), let $S\subseteq\mathbb N$, let $N\ge 1$ and $r\ge 1$, let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$) and $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid N$, $q\notin S$) — to the residue field of $\mathcal O$, let $q_1,\dots,q_t$ be nonzero naturals with scalars $\alpha_i$ in that residue field, put $L=N(\prod_i q_i)r$, let $H\le(\mathbb Z/L)^\times$ be a subgroup, and assume `OpComm` and `DiaComm`, i.e. the Hecke operators on the localised cohomology module commute among themselves and with the diamond operators [`CohCarrier.diamondL`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L55). Let $\mathcal O'$ be a second such discrete valuation ring, an $\mathcal O$-algebra via a local homomorphism, and let $\lambda$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism from the Hecke ring `HeckeRing` (the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of endomorphisms of that module generated by the $T_\ell$ and the diamonds $\langle u\rangle$, $u\in H_r:=\ker((\mathbb Z/L)^\times\to(\mathbb Z/r)^\times)$) to $\mathcal O'$. Then there is a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation ring $\mathcal O_1$ with finite residue field, finite as a module over $\mathcal O'$, with $\mathcal O'\to\mathcal O_1$ local and injective, such that one of two alternatives holds. Either there are a Dirichlet character $\varepsilon$ modulo $L$ over $\mathbb C$, a weight-two cusp form $h$ on $\Gamma_1(L)$ and a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C$ generated by all $q$-expansion coefficients of $h$ together with all values $\varepsilon(u)$ into $\mathcal O_1$, with [`CuspForm.IsEigenformWith ε h`](def/CuspForm_PrimitiveFormGamma1.html#L19) (normalisation $a_1(h)=1$, the Hecke multiplicativity relations at primes $p\nmid L$ and $\ell\mid L$, and the nebentypus transformation law under $\Gamma_0(L)$), with $\varepsilon$ trivial on $H$, with $\iota(a_\ell(h))$ the image of $\lambda(T_\ell)$ for every prime $\ell\notin S$ not dividing $L$, and with $\iota(\varepsilon(u))$ the image of $\lambda(\langle u\rangle)$ for every $u\in H_r$; or there are Dirichlet characters $\psi_1,\psi_2$ modulo $L$ and a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra generated by their values into $\mathcal O_1$ with $\psi_1\psi_2$ trivial on $H$, $\iota(\psi_1(\ell))+\ell\,\iota(\psi_2(\ell))$ equal to the image of $\lambda(T_\ell)$ for all primes $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid L$ (evaluating at the unit class of $\ell$), and $\iota(\psi_1(u))\,\iota(\psi_2(u))$ equal to the image of $\lambda(\langle u\rangle)$ for $u\in H_r$.
--
--   This is the classicality statement for a point of the Hecke ring acting on the localised first cohomology of $\Gamma_H(L)$ at a Taylor–Wiles level: such a point comes, after a finite local extension of coefficients, either from a normalised weight-two eigenform with nebentypus on $\Gamma_1(L)$ or from an Eisenstein pair of Dirichlet characters. It is the modular-forms half of the construction of the $\lambda$-adic Galois representation attached to such a point, and is used for that construction and for the ensuing local statements at primes dividing the level and at the auxiliary Taylor–Wiles primes.
